Article 5 A People's Court shall not seal up, distrain, or freeze the following property of a person subject to enforcement:
(1) Clothing, furniture, cooking utensils, tableware, and other articles necessary for the daily life of the person subject to enforcement and his/her dependents;
(2) Necessary living expenses for the person subject to enforcement and his/her dependents. If the locality has a minimum subsistence guarantee standard, the necessary living expenses shall be determined in accordance with such standard;
(3) Articles necessary for the person subject to enforcement and his/her dependents to complete compulsory education;
(4) Unpublished inventions or unpublished works;
(5) Assistive devices and medical articles necessary for physical disabilities of the person subject to enforcement and his/her dependents;
(6) Medals and other articles of honor awarded to the person subject to enforcement;
(7) Property that is exempted from sealing up, distraining, or freezing under treaties, agreements, and other documents of a treaty or agreement nature concluded with foreign countries or international organizations in the name of the People's Republic of China, the government of the People's Republic of China, or departments of the government of the People's Republic of China, in accordance with the Law of the People's Republic of China on the Procedure of Concluding Treaties;
(8) Other property that shall not be sealed up, distrained, or frozen as provided for by laws or judicial interpretations.

Article 20 When sealing up, distraining, or freezing the property of the person subject to enforcement, the enforcement personnel shall make a record stating the following:
(1) The time when the enforcement measure commenced and was completed;
(2) The location, type, and quantity of the property;
(3) The custodian of the property;
(4) Other matters that shall be recorded.
The enforcement personnel and the custodian shall sign the record; if persons specified in Article 224 of the Civil Procedure Law are present, those present shall also sign the record.

Article 31 Under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall make a ruling to release the sealing up, distraining, or freezing and serve it on the person applying for enforcement, the person subject to enforcement, or an interested person:
(1) The property of an interested person has been sealed up, distrained, or frozen;
(2) The person applying for enforcement withdraws the enforcement application or waives the creditor's right;
(3) The sealed up, distrained, or frozen property fails to sell at auction or by other means, and the person applying for enforcement and other enforcement creditors do not agree to accept the property in satisfaction of the debt;
(4) The debt has been satisfied;
(5) The person subject to enforcement provides security and the person applying for enforcement agrees to release the sealing up, distraining, or freezing;
(6) Other circumstances under which the People's Court deems that the sealing up, distraining, or freezing shall be released.
If the sealing up, distraining, or freezing was implemented by means of registration, a notice of assistance in enforcement shall be sent to the registration authority for the release.
